##########################################################################################
# Tackling Real-World Data Integration Challenges through Generic RML Extensions         #
##########################################################################################
<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/elsdvlee/papers/main/2026/Tackling_Real-World_Data_Integration_Challenges_through_Generic_RML_Extensions.pdf> 
    a schema:ScholarlyArticle;
    schema:name          "Tackling Real-World Data Integration Challenges through Generic RML Extensions" ;
    schema:abstract
    """
    To construct RDF Knowledge Graphs (KGs), heterogeneous
    source data can be systematically transformed and published in downstream 
    systems. Declarative mapping languages enable generic, transparent, and maintainable 
    KG construction. However, real-world data systems require 
    access control for different levels of granularity, 
    apply data references across and within data sources, and apply deeply-nested
    and mixed-format data structures. These realities cannot be handled in
    current mapping languages, which hinders their broader adoption and
    leads practitioners to rely on custom pipelines. This PhD research aims
    to improve KG construction to support the aforementioned realities in
    the RDF Mapping Language (RML), a mature declarative mapping language. 
    Guided by the Design-oriented Information Systems Research
    methodology, I identify functional gaps in RML (namely, data-driven
    publication, explicit relations across and within data sources, integration 
    of individual data values from other data sources, integration of
    mixed-format data, and references to structural positions) while working 
    on data integration projects, specify and implement RML extensions,
    evaluate their effectiveness and efficiency, and disseminate them for feedback, 
    adoption, and standardization. As preliminary findings I introduced 
    (i) RML Dynamic Targets for data-driven publication in different
    levels of granularity (e.g., per-entity or per-policy) and (ii) RML Reference 
    Conditions to improve performance when referencing data across
    data sources (e.g., decreasing external RML engine Morph-KGC’s execution 
    time by 12%). I am currently elaborating RML Logical Views,
    an expressive data access description, integrating data value integration,
    mixed-format data structures, and structural position references. My involvement 
    in the W3C KG Construction Community allows me to validate 
    the proposed solutions with a broad set of real-world use cases, and
    the integration of my solutions in an existing and mature specification
    increases the impact of my work. An important risk in my research is
    managing its scope, as each new use case may reveal additional gaps. I
    will focus on the set of gaps identified so far as the minimal viable scope
    required to meaningfully complete this PhD.
    """;
    schema:datePublished "2026-04-29"^^xsd:date;
    schema:about  <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Semantic_Web> ;
    schema:author <https://elsdvlee.pod.knows.idlab.ugent.be/profile/card#me>;
